WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.429 --> 00:00:06.049
Post-credits scenes are all the rage these
days.

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:10.240
But before those became popular, some movies
included jokes or hidden references within

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.299
the actual credits themselves.

00:00:12.299 --> 00:00:15.609
Here are some of our favorites from new and
old movies alike.

00:00:15.609 --> 00:00:17.119
Frozen boogers

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:19.279
Being a Disney hero is hungry work.

00:00:19.279 --> 00:00:23.590
Luckily, Kristoff in Frozen found the perfect
snack to fortify him: his own boogers.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:26.180
“What if you hate the way he picks his nose?”

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.330
“Picks his nose?”

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:28.750
“And eats it.”

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.580
“Excuse me sir, he is a prince.”

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.190
“All men do it.”

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:34.190
“Ewww”

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.260
Even Disney thought that was a bit gross,
though, adding this disclaimer to the credits:

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.470
"the views and opinions expressed by Kristoff
in this film that all men eat their own boogers

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:46.309
are solely his and do not necessarily reflect
the views or opinions of The Walt Disney Company

00:00:46.309 --> 00:00:47.670
or the filmmakers."

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:48.670
Do no harm

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.680
When a movie involves any kinds of animals
or creatures, it's always important for the

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.329
filmmakers to ensure the critters' safety.

00:00:56.329 --> 00:00:58.850
Even if those animals are CGI.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.350
Pay close attention to the credits in Guardians
of the Galaxy and you'll see confirmation

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.420
that no raccoons or tree creatures were in
any way harmed during the production of the

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:07.420
film.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.500
In Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire, we
learn that "no dragons were harmed in the

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:11.790
making of this movie."

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:15.370
And Mr. Popper's Penguins assures us that
that no penguins were harmed, but, "Jim Carrey,

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.670
on the other hand, was bitten mercilessly.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.470
But he had it coming."

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.130
No argument here.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.619
Meanwhile, The Naked Gun flicks apparently
revelled in animal cruelty, claiming in the

00:01:33.619 --> 00:01:38.180
credits that some entire species went extinct
during principal photography.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:39.920
“Nice beaver.”

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.300
“Thank you, I just had it stuffed.”

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:46.180
"Let me help you with that."

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.381
Coffee shop

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.180
When the Supreme Court ruled that corporations
are people, they based their decision on Pulp

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.180
Fiction.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.180
Okay, that’s not true.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:54.470
But it could've been.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:55.470
Why?

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:58.710
During the robbery scene, the restaurant manager’s
dialogue was so obscured by screaming and

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.080
swearing that all he could get out was this:

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.820
“I am not a hero, I am just a coffee shop…”

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:09.299
So in the credits, Quentin Tarantino decided
to simply name him “Coffee Shop.”

00:02:09.299 --> 00:02:10.330
Cozy in the closet

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:14.749
The end credits of Harry Potter and the Prisoner
of Azkaban gives viewers a long look at the

00:02:14.749 --> 00:02:19.029
infamous Marauder's Map, which shows the layout
of Hogwarts and all the folks sneaking around

00:02:19.029 --> 00:02:20.489
its hallways and rooms.

00:02:20.489 --> 00:02:23.859
For a moment during the credits, however,
we see two pairs of feet in the corner of

00:02:23.859 --> 00:02:27.700
the screen that don't really sneak so much
as they seem like they might be…knocking

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:28.700
boots?

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.450
Who do you think it might be?

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.409
Our money is on Professor McGonagall and Professor
Flitwick.

00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:34.409
Yikes.

00:02:34.409 --> 00:02:36.069
Hello Hanso

00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:40.450
Director J.J. Abrams doesn't shy away from
throwing fun Easter eggs into his projects.

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.090
Usually it’s just Greg Grunberg.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:46.560
But during the credits of 2006's Mission:
Impossible III, the filmmakers give a "special

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.260
thanks" to the Hanso Foundation.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.100
Of course, true fans will recognize the Hanso
Foundation as the mysterious group that had

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:56.579
a hand in all the crazy island shenanigans
on Abrams' television project, Lost.

00:02:56.579 --> 00:03:00.370
Maybe Jack and the rest of the gang got stranded
all because of that damn rabbit's foot…

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:02.169
“Where is the rabbit's foot?”

00:03:02.169 --> 00:03:04.779
You have 15 seconds to comply

00:03:04.779 --> 00:03:08.510
Most movies contain a warning that the film
is protected under copyright laws in the United

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.370
States, followed by some legal mumbo-jumbo.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:14.579
It's the same disclaimer every time—except
in the case of Robocop.

00:03:14.579 --> 00:03:17.950
“I’d buy that for a dollar.”

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:21.849
At the end of the Paul Verhoeven film, the
disclaimer reads, "unauthorized duplication,

00:03:21.849 --> 00:03:26.290
distribution or exhibition may result in civil
liability and criminal prosecution by enforcement

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:27.290
droids."

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:30.489
In other words, pirate Robocop and you may
get a visit from ED-209.

00:03:30.489 --> 00:03:32.430
Thanks for watching!

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:35.939
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:35.939 --> 00:03:39.239
And leave a comment to let us know which movies
you think should've made the list...

